[codex] ## Session Runtime Discipline
[codex] 
[codex] Your session is a bounded process: a hard deadline is enforced by a
[codex] process-group kill that takes down EVERY process you started (including
[codex] training/evaluation jobs). Only files on disk survive. These rules exist
[codex] because each has destroyed a real session before:
[codex] 
[codex] - Never run a command expected to exceed ~10 minutes in the foreground.
[codex]   Launch it detached (plain `nohup ... > <log> 2>&1 &` — never `setsid`),
[codex]   record its PID to a file, then poll in BOUNDED waits (each ≤5 minutes),
[codex]   doing useful work between polls. Note: a blocking foreground call is
[codex]   auto-backgrounded by the CLI after ~2 minutes and you get its output-file
[codex]   path — poll that file; but prefer explicit nohup so the pattern is under
[codex]   your control.
[codex] - WATCHER DISCIPLINE: at most ONE watcher/timer per awaited artifact.
[codex]   Before spawning a replacement, kill the superseded watcher by its
[codex]   recorded PID. Prefer a single until-condition wait over chains of
[codex]   sleep-watchers.
[codex] - TIMERS: the ScheduleWakeup tool is disabled here — its wakeups never
[codex]   fire in this session mode. For a timed check-back, launch a background
[codex]   task as your alarm clock: `sleep <seconds> && echo ALARM` — its
[codex]   completion notification wakes you.
[codex] - DEAD-MAN'S ALARM: never end a turn whose only pending wake sources are
[codex]   condition-watchers (a watcher with a buggy condition can hang forever,
[codex]   and silence looks identical to "still waiting"). Keep one bounded
[codex]   background alarm task (e.g. `sleep 600 && echo fallback-tick`) alive
[codex]   whenever you are purely waiting, so no wedged watcher can strand you for
[codex]   more than ~10 minutes.
[codex] - A completion notification is EVIDENCE, not noise: before dismissing one
[codex]   as stale, read the file or task output it names. Your belief that you
[codex]   killed a process does not outrank a result file on disk.
[codex] - KILL DISCIPLINE: terminate processes by specific PID only. NEVER use
[codex]   pattern kills (`pkill -f python`, `pkill -f <server>`) or group kills
[codex]   (`kill 0`, `kill -- -PID`): this machine also runs YOUR OWN session and
[codex]   its orchestrator, and a pattern/group kill will terminate you mid-work.
[codex] - NO ORPHANED VALUE: every background job you start must end in one of two
[codex]   recorded states — CONSUMED (its output read and used/logged) or
[codex]   ABANDONED (a written note saying why). Before your session ends, sweep
[codex]   for finished or still-running background work whose results you have
[codex]   not consumed (a grown artifact, an unread eval) and consume or promote
[codex]   it; work products with nobody left alive to use them are lost value.
[codex] - Persist partial progress incrementally (append/save every few minutes
[codex]   of work) so a kill never loses more than one interval.
